<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">As an influential information fusion and low-level vision technique, image fusion integrates complementary information from source images to yield an informative fused image. A few attempts <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">have been</span> made in recent years to jointly realize image fusion and super-resolution. However, in real-world applications such as military reconnaissance and
long-range detection missions, the target and background structures in multimodal images are easily corrupted, with low resolution and weak semantic information, which <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">leads to</span> suboptimal results in current fusion techniques. In response, we propose FS-Diff, a semantic guidance and clarity-aware joint image fusion and super-resolution method. FS-Diff unifies image fusion and super-resolution as a conditional generation problem<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. It leverages</span> semantic guidance from the proposed clarity sensing mechanism for adaptive low-resolution perception and cross-modal feature extraction. Specifically, we initialize the desired fused result as pure Gaussian noise and introduce the bidirectional feature Mamba to extract <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the</span> global features of the multimodal images. <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Moreover</span>, utilizing the source images and semantics as conditions, we implement a random iterative denoising process <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">via</span> a modified U-Net network<span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This network is </span>trained for denoising at multiple noise levels to produce high-resolution fusion results with cross-modal features and abundant semantic information. We also construct a
powerful aerial view multiscene (AVMS) benchmark <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">covering</span> 859 pairs of images. Extensive joint image fusion and super-resolution experiments on six public and our AVMS datasets demonstrated that FS-Diff <span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">outperforms</span> the state-of-the-art methods at multiple magnifications and can recover richer details and semantics in the fused images. The code is available at https://github.com/XylonXu01/FS-Diff.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>
Image fusion, image super-resolution, diffusion model, vision language model, benchmark

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Owing to the limitations of optical imaging systems, images obtained <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">via</span> a single imaging device can only characterize part of a scene. For example, infrared (IR) images <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">are</span> limited by <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">their</span> specific spectral range and imaging resolution<span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. They</span> can present <span id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">only</span> some of the thermal radiation characteristics <span id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">of</span> a target and cannot reflect the texture and details of the visible image (VI). Multimodal image fusion (MMIF) is an essential field within image processing<span id="S1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. It focuses</span> on integrating complementary information from diverse sensors to generate a fusion result with a more complete representation of the scene <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Fused images with superior visual perception and excellent scene and target representation are widely used in fields such as biometrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, semantic segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and target detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Medical image fusion (MIF) and visible and infrared image fusion (VIRF) are challenging MMIF tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">MIF can simultaneously represent the metabolic and structural information <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">in</span> functional and anatomical medical images. <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">By</span> contrast, VIRF aims to preserve the high-spatial-resolution texture information in VI and the thermal target information in IR, <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">as IR</span> is not sensitive to light. In the past few decades, various MMIF techniques have been developed to enhance the quality of fused images. These fusion approaches can be classified as multiscale transform (MST)-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, sparse representation (SR)-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, saliency detection (SD)-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and deep learning (DL)-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the first three conventional fusion methods, DL-based methods can learn meaningful features from extensive training data to meet the requirements of sensitivity and fast adaptation of fusion methods in complex scenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Although existing MMIF can achieve satisfactory fusion outcomes in general, the following two challenges are neglected in practical applications. First, existing fusion methods suffer from poor performance in adaptively processing low-resolution (LR) source images, making it difficult to fulfill the requirements of applications such as unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) reconnaissance, crop monitoring, and autonomous driving. In these applications, the state of the target or camera changes rapidly, <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">increasing the demand for</span> image information and resolution. In particular, when UAVs <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">capture images</span> at high altitudes, multiple factors, such as flight altitude, steadiness, and atmospheric conditions, significantly affect image clarity. Therefore, in unfavorable imaging environments, such as long-distance shooting, and under the performance limitations of imaging systems, it is <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">crucial</span> to investigate methods that can adaptively perceive resolution degradation and flexibly incorporate semantic information while preserving cross-modal features. <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This is a challenge that urgently needs to be solved.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09427v1/01.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/321;" width="476" height="321" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Our FS-Diff demonstrates the outstanding fusion performance of joint image fusion and super-resolution with a scaling factor of 8 in <span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> public and our AVMS datasets. <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The first four boxes show the VI-IR dataset, demonstrating three fusion scenarios: both VI and IR blurred, VI blurred, and IR blurred. The remaining two boxes present the multimodal medical dataset, where both images are blurred.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Generally</span>, image fusion and super-resolution tasks can only be completed in steps<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. However, this approach</span> <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">can amplify errors from previous steps in subsequent steps, leading to a serious loss of modal information.</span> Despite <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">efforts</span> to solve this problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, several challenges <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">still</span> remain. Xiao et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> proposed a heterogeneous knowledge distillation network model<span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. It</span> attempts to obtain high-resolution (HR) fused images by using a teacher network to direct the student network through a corner-embedded attention mechanism. Li et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> proposed a meta-learning-based fusion framework <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">for fusing</span> images with different resolutions. However, their generalizability and flexibility <span id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">are limited</span> on datasets of different types, failing to meet the high-magnification requirements of real scenes. When <span id="S1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">processing</span> LR images with severely degraded resolution, these methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> struggle to adaptively perceive resolution and incorporate semantic information into the fused results. They also lack robust global and cross-modal feature extraction capabilities, making the fusion results susceptible to artifacts and hindering the recovery of high-fidelity details. This is mainly because high-magnification joint image fusion and super-resolution <span id="S1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">require</span> not only pixel value adjustment but also full consideration of image semantics, details, and textures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Second,  <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">despite advancements in specialized applications, existing aerial photography datasets present inherent limitations that hinder their direct adaptation to the MMIF task. For example, DroneVehicle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> provides RGB-IR image pairs in diverse lighting conditions but lacks object diversity. Anti-UAV410 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> only provides thermal infrared unimodal data and lacks visible counterparts. VEDAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> is limited by confined lighting conditions and a narrow range of target categories.</span> <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Furthermore, although</span>  existing <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">MMIF</span> datasets <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">including</span> TNO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, RoadScene <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, LLVIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, M<sup id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>FD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and MSRS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> are widely used, they are not specifically constructed for the dual tasks of simultaneous image super-resolution and fusion in long-distance shooting (<span id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, aerial photography using a UAV). Specifically, these datasets <span id="S1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">lack coverage of degraded-resolution aerial images, diverse shooting distances, varied viewpoints, and a wide range of scenes.</span> Additionally, <span id="S1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">MMIF</span> has the potential to address the inconsistent performance of unimodal images in detection and segmentation tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. However, owing to the lack of annotated targets in available datasets, assess<span id="S1.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ing</span> the effectiveness of fusion approaches based on the performance of fused images in target detection or semantic segmentation tasks <span id="S1.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">is difficult for the researchers</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Generative models are widely used in DL-based MMIF owing to their superior visual perception. <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">These</span> models effective<span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">ly</span> <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">analyze</span> the empirical distributions of complex scenes through <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">an in-depth examination of the</span> intrinsic laws and statistical properties of the images. Among these, generative adversarial networks (GANs)-based image fusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> are predominant. Such models typically consist of a generator that is dedicated to fusion image generation and a discriminator that evaluates the distribution similarity between the fused result and the source image. Despite the potential of GAN-based methods for generating satisfying fusion results, they experience unstable optimization, and their model-building process requires careful regularization and optimization strategies. In addition, GAN-based methods often <span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">face</span> issues <span id="S1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">such as</span> mode collapse and training instability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. As a successor to the GAN model, the denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> recovers superior images via diffusion and denoising processes. Compared with GAN, DDPM exhibits a steadier optimization process and stronger interpretability. Zhao et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> designed the fusion method utilizing DDPM<span id="S1.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which</span> splits the conditional generation problem into a maximum likelihood subproblem and an unconditional generation subproblem<span id="S1.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This provides</span> a new idea for the design of fusion methods based on generative models. <span id="S1.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Beyond</span> these advancements, <span id="S1.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">integrating generative models</span> with vision-language model (VLM) offers new opportunities to enhance the quality of generated images. VLM can more precisely understand and generate content that aligns with specific semantics. By combining the strengths of the generative model with VLM, the field of MMIF <span id="S1.p6.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">can be advanced further</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we propose a novel approach called FS-Diff<span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. It</span> effectively utilizes generative priors and semantics to guide the model for clarity adaptive perception and intra- and inter-modal feature extraction to jointly achieve MMIF and super-resolution. FS-Diff is adaptive <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">for sensing</span> the degradation of resolution, with multi<span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">modal</span> learning capabilities, and includes forward and backward Markovian diffusion processes. The forward Markovian process gradually adds Gaussian noise to the HR fusion image obtained by fusing the HR source images using CDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite><span id="S1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. This process</span> efficiently processes multimodal information, captures deep correlations between modalities, and achieves good fusion results. The reverse Markovian process connects the noisy target image of the forward process with the LR source image<span id="S1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span> <span id="S1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">It</span> constructs a joint representation of the multimodal data in the latent space by introducing a bidirectional feature Mamba (BFM) block <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite><span id="S1.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">,</span> <span id="S1.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">which</span> can perceive global information to train a modified U-Net network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> to predict and eliminate the different levels of noise added by the forward process to recover the original HR fusion image. The inference phase starts with pure Gaussian noise<span id="S1.p7.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The</span> HR fusion results are obtained by iteratively refining the noise removal by using a denoising network with multiple noise removal capabilities, conditioned on multimodal LR images and semantic guidance. <span id="S1.p7.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To achieve precise semantic embedding during fusion, we aggregate semantic extraction and clarity perception capabilities through the proposed clarity sensing (CLSE) mechanism and the clarity-aware contrastive language-image pre-training (CA-CLIP) model.</span> Through the cross attention module, deep semantics are effectively injected into <span id="S1.p7.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the</span> fusion features. The combination of the generative model with BFM and CLSE significantly enhances the ability of FS-Diff to handle cross-modal and detail feature extraction in the presence of image resolution degradation. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, FS-Diff has high joint fusion and super-resolution performance with good generalization on VIRF, MIF, and multifocus tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this paper are as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an end-to-end semantic-guided and resolution-aware fusion network. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">study to achieve synchronous fusion and super-resolution of pairs of LR multimodal image pairs with inconsistent clarity</span> at multiple high magnifications.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">introduce the</span> CLSE mechanism, where the core component is our proposed CA-CLIP, <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">which is</span> endowed with a clarity-adaptive sensing capability to extract semantic information from source images in advance to guide the fusion process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the BFM to create a consolidated joint image representation during the fusion process to model and extract global features from multimodal images.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct an aerial view multiscene (AVMS) dataset with 859 aligned VI and IR image pairs, 3821 annotated targets that cover daytime, nighttime, dusk, and complex weather conditions, and more than seven different scenes. Our dataset is suitable for image fusion, super-resolution, long-distance detection, and semantic segmentation tasks.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 presents a review of the research background. Section 3 details the motivation and algorithmic specifics of FS-Diff. Section 4 describes the created AVMS dataset. Section 5 presents the experimental validations. Section 6 compares the computational complexity of FS-Diff with those of other methods. Section 7 concludes the paper by outlining the contributions and application prospects.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Image fusion methods</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1 </span>Traditional fusion methods</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional image fusion methods include MST-based, SR-based, and SD-based <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">approaches</span>. Among these, MST-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> include three steps: decomposition, fusion, and reconstruction. First, typical MST tools, such as non-subsampled contourlet transforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> and edge-preserving filters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, are introduced to decompose the image into multiscale components. Subsequently, specific fusion rules are used to measure the pixel activity of the image, and pixels or regions with high activity are selected. Finally, the fused image is reconstructed using the inverse MST. MST-based methods can reduce the generation of artifacts during the fusion process owing to translation invariance. However, <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">inappropriate selection of</span> the decomposition tool or number of decomposition layers will lead to a loss of information in the fused image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SR achieves an efficient and accurate representation of the image by decomposing it into a linear combination of sparse coefficients and an overcomplete dictionary. SR-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> assume that the activity level of an image can be computed in a sparse domain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. They first segment the image into multiple overlapping blocks via a sliding window, vectorize them<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Then, they</span> compute the sparse coefficients using sparse coding, and finally fuse them to reconstruct the fused image. Although SR-based methods have better noise robustness, balancing fusion performance and efficiency remains a challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">SD mimics the visual mechanism to highlight regions of an image that are significant to the surroundings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">It</span> is widely used in tasks such as image compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> and segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. SD-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> use saliency extraction operators to identify the salient regions of a source image, which in turn generates a decision map to reconstruct the result. These methods can be further classified into two categories namely, salient object detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> and salient edge detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, both of which consider the correlation between pixels in a local region. However, SD-based methods are sensitive to significance-detection algorithms and poor significance-extraction operators can lead to discontinuities at the regional junctions of the fused image.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2 </span>Deep learning-based methods</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, DL-based methods aimed at enhancing the perception of fused images, such as contrast and color, have emerged <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>. These methods can learn more effective fusion strategies under the constraint of a loss function owing to their powerful nonlinear fitting performance and learning abilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. DL-based methods primarily include autoencoder(AE) model-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, convolutional neural network (CNN)-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, and generation model (GEM)-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The AE-based method compresses the input data into a low-dimensional feature representation through an encoder and designs fusion layer rules to extract the features<span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. These features</span> are then remapped back into the data space by a decoder to complete image reconstruction. DenseFuse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> is a typical AE-based method that trains a self-encoder using the MS-COCO dataset while combining addition and L1 norm strategies to obtain fused images. CDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> uses a self-coding network with a two-branch transformer-CNN structure and combines it with feature decomposition loss to extract cross-modal information.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09427v1/02.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/389;" width="476" height="389" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The framework of FS-Diff. <math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the noisy output image with <span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">t</span> timesteps and <span id="S2.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the ground truth</span>. The forward diffusion process <math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{t-1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{t-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> adds Gaussian noise to <math id="S2.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the backward diffusion process <math id="S2.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}|\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}|\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math> iteratively denoise. <span id="S2.F2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The CLSE mechanism dynamically selects semantic extraction strategies for multimodal images with inconsistent resolution.</span> <span id="S2.F2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In Case 1 (single-image blur), CA-CLIP’s image encoder injects the content embedding extracted from the clear image into the diffusion process. For Case 2 (dual-image blur), the model instead injects the maximum cross-image content embedding that is obtained from both blurred inputs.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">CNN-based methods utilize a well-designed network structure with loss functions to complete image feature extraction, fusion, and reconstruction. PMGI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>, as a representative method, divides the network into gradient and intensity paths to maintain the ratio of texture to intensity information in the fused image, and it reuses the features in the same path. To further improve the interpretability of the algorithm, LRRNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> achieves fusion by mathematically modelling and analyzing the fusion task and establishing a link between the optimal solution and the network that implements it.<span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> CoCoNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> employs a coupled contrastive constraint loss and multilevel attention modules to preserve hierarchical features of different modalities, mitigating artifacts in the fused results. Text-IF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite> leverages semantic text guidance to achieve degradation-aware and interactive image fusion.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">GEM-based methods, particularly GAN- and diffusion-model-based fusion methods, generate fused images that approximate the source image distribution through adversarial training and probabilistic modeling strategies, respectively. Typical methods, such as TCGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite> combine a conditional GAN and a transformer and design a wavelet fusion module to fuse cross-domain features. DDFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> decomposes the fusion task into two subproblems, namely, maximum likelihood and unconditional generation based on Bayesian modeling<span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. It</span> obtains the fused image <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">using</span> a natural image generation prior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, to meet the demands of advanced visual tasks, scholars have explored task-oriented image fusion methods<span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span> <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">These methods</span> apply high-level model semantic constraints to guide the training of fusion networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>. <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To enhance fusion image quality and the accuracy of subsequent perception tasks, CAF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite> introduces a perception-driven loss function, formulating fusion and perception as a joint problem. MRFS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> enhances VIRF and segmentation performance by coupling these tasks and leveraging the intrinsic consistency between visual and semantic information.</span> DetFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite> guides image fusion using target information obtained from target-detection network learning. Tang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite> proposed combining fusion and semantic segmentation modules and increasing the semantic information obtained by the fusion module through semantic loss. Both methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite> guided the fusion process by using information on the target location and semantic categories to improve the quality of the fused images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Although the aforementioned methods can achieve satisfactory fusion results, they still face significant challenges in practical applications, particularly when dealing with LR MMIF. To address this challenge, some scholars have conducted preliminary studies on practice-oriented image fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>. Yin et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> proposed the interpolation of LR source images before fusing them by using an SR-based approach. Subsequently, Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> proposed the joint learning of low-rank, sparse, and transformation dictionaries as well as learning compensation dictionaries that can reduce the loss of structure in the fused image and increase the loss of information during image super-resolution. However, this dual-task step-by-step processing method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> has high computational complexity<span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span> <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">It may also amplify artifacts generated in the super-resolution stage during the fusion stage</span>. Moreover, this method <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">cannot handle image fusion with high scaling factors.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Text to image models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VLMs offer significant opportunities for improving tasks involving general text and visual representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>. These models typically consist of a text encoder and an image encoder, which leverage contrastive learning to capture the aligned multimodal features between text and images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite>. As a typical example of VLMs, CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite> employs image and text encoders and contrastive loss to align image-text pairs. It demonstrates robust zero-shot learning capabilities in diverse downstream tasks <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">because of</span> its unsupervised training method and construction of large-scale datasets. To further enhance CLIP’s performance on image restoration tasks, DA-CLIP emerged. DA-CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite> integrates VLMs with low-level visual tasks, focusing on controlling the pretrained CLIP to predict degradation types and accordingly restore high-quality image features. CLIP-KD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite> enhances the performance of student models in zero-shot classification and cross-modal retrieval tasks through feature mimicry and interactive contrastive learning between the student and teacher encoders. Additionally, Stable Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>]</cite> achieves text-controlled image generation through the integration of attention and text encoding. By leveraging text guidance, it enables the customization of image generation, facilitating interactive control of multimodal fusion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Diffusion models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> have been widely used in image super-resolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>, <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite>, image editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>, <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite> and image inpainting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite> owing to their powerful generative abilities. In addition, the latent features of the data learned by the diffusion model during training can be used for discriminative tasks, such as image segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>, <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> and classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite>. The central goal is to learn to recover the structure of the training data disturbed by noise via forward and backward diffusion processes. The forward process gradually adds different levels of noise to the training data over multiple time steps, whereas the reverse process learns an iterative denoising process via U-Net to recover the original image from Gaussian noise. The inference phase begins with Gaussian noise and iteratively estimates and removes noise to obtain the target output <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>. Similar to Langevin dynamics, the conditional diffusion probabilistic model (C-DDPM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>]</cite> is based on conditional inputs and transforms from a standard normal distribution to an empirical data distribution through a multistage refinement process. DiffiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite> achieves efficient high-fidelity image generation by introducing a time-dependent multihead self-attention mechanism for fine-grained control of the denoising process. <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The application of</span> diffusion models to image fusion <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">is promising</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Diffusion models have been widely applied in MMIF. Dif-Fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite> pioneered the integration of diffusion models into VIRF tasks, enhancing the color fidelity of the fused images. To optimize the efficient mapping of the original resolution image features in MMIF, LFDT-Fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite> introduced a latent feature-guided diffusion model for image fusion. Diff-IF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite> presented a fusion method based on a diffusion model and incorporating fusion knowledge priors. By decomposing the diffusion model into a conditional diffusion component and a fusion knowledge prior module, it facilitates the generation of high-fidelity fused results. Additionally, DRMF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite> proposed a degradation-robust conditional diffusion model to mitigate the impact of image degradation on the MMIF. However,</span> current image fusion methods <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">based on diffusion models</span> are ineffective under conditions of single or dual-source image resolution degradation and neglect the preservation of semantic information. Moreover, MMIF and super-resolution are deterministic image-generation processes, and the output of a well-designed model should maintain a high degree of similarity to the source images. Therefore, <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">utilizing</span> the powerful generative capabilities of DDPM combined with VLM <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">is essential</span> in the fusion process. This allows the model to adaptively recognize resolution degradation and generate semantically rich fused images, thereby enhancing the accuracy of advanced visual tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Mamba</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The Mamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>]</cite> model has the potential for long-sequence modeling, state space models (SSMs)-based models are motivated by continuous systems. It maps a one-dimensional sequence <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t)\in\mathbb{R}\rightarrow y(t)\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t)\in\mathbb{R}\rightarrow y(t)\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> through hidden states <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(t)\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The representation of the continuous system is as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}(t)=\bm{A}h(t)+\bm{B}x(t),\quad y(t)=\bm{C}h(t)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}(t)=\bm{A}h(t)+\bm{B}x(t),\quad y(t)=\bm{C}h(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> is the evolution parameter, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑪</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> are projection parameters.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To implement these systems in deep learning, the continuous parameters are transformed into discrete forms using a discretization step. A timescale parameter <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is introduced to compute discrete versions of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\bm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\bm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The Zero-Order Hold method is employed for transformation, defined as:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\bm{A}}=\exp(\Delta\bm{A}),\quad\overline{\bm{B}}=(\Delta\bm{A})^{-1}(\exp(\Delta\bm{A})-\bm{I})\cdot\Delta\bm{B}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\bm{A}}=\exp(\Delta\bm{A}),\quad\overline{\bm{B}}=(\Delta\bm{A})^{-1}(\exp(\Delta\bm{A})-\bm{I})\cdot\Delta\bm{B}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Using discrete parameters, the continuous equations are reformulated into the following discrete-time version:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}=\overline{\bm{A}}h_{t-1}+\overline{\bm{B}}x_{t},\quad y_{t}={\bm{C}}h_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">x</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">y</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}=\overline{\bm{A}}h_{t-1}+\overline{\bm{B}}x_{t},\quad y_{t}={\bm{C}}h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finally, the models output results through global convolution:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{K}}=(\overline{\bm{C}}\overline{\bm{B}},{\bm{C}}\overline{\bm{A}}\overline{\bm{B}},\dots,{\bm{C}}\overline{\bm{A}}^{M-1}\overline{\bm{B}}),\ \mathbf{y}=\mathbf{x}*\overline{\mathbf{K}}\ " display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐊</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑪</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐲</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐊</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{K}}=(\overline{\bm{C}}\overline{\bm{B}},{\bm{C}}\overline{\bm{A}}\overline{\bm{B}},\dots,{\bm{C}}\overline{\bm{A}}^{M-1}\overline{\bm{B}}),\ \mathbf{y}=\mathbf{x}*\overline{\mathbf{K}}\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{K}}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐊</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{K}}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the structured convolution kernel and <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the length of the input sequence <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Motivation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although existing approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">102</a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite> generally focus on improving the network’s ability to extract detailed features from multi<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">modal</span> images, the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">MMIF</span> task still faces three prominent challenges. First, in UAV aerial photography and fast unmanned scenarios, the problems of image structure destruction and resolution degradation due to long distances are particularly prominent. LR images not only weaken the clarity of the background and target in the fused image but also restrict the performance of the fused image in subsequent segmentation and detection tasks, thus restricting the scope of practical applications of image fusion. Current fusion methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite> for LR images are only effective at low magnifications and cannot adapt to the serious degradation of image resolution,<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">making</span> it difficult to meet the performance and flexibility requirements in real scenes. Second, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">owing</span> to the inherent variations across modalities in multimodal images, an effective fusion method that preserves both global and semantic information is crucial. However, current fusion techniques fail to adequately address these challenges, resulting in feature loss and diminished accuracy in subsequent visual tasks. Third, dataset construction is key to implementing the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">MMIF</span> method in applications. From the UAV perspective, changes in flight altitude lead to variable image field-of-view sizes, which, in turn, cause variability in target sizes. This complexity further exacerbates the challenges of visual tasks, such as image fusion, semantic segmentation, and target detection, under multiple lighting conditions. However, the current publicly available <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">MMIF</span> datasets are not only limited in number but <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">are</span> also unavailable from aerial viewpoints, which severely limits the possibility of unmanned aerial systems realizing multi<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">modal</span> heterogeneous data fusion functions in agriculture, military, and other applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If the aforementioned challenges are not fully considered and effectively addressed, the performance of fusion algorithms in real applications will degrade significantly. Therefore, we propose FS-Diff. The core of FS-Diff is the use of the LR input image and the prior semantics obtained by the presented <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">CLSE with</span> CA-CLIP to constrain the diffusion process, which alleviates the problems of the unstable training and performance degradation of DL-based methods . <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Moreover</span>, to enhance the global and cross-modal feature perception of the fusion algorithm for multi<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">modal</span> images, we introduce the BFM  to construct a joint representation of multi<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">modal</span> images.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>FS-Diff</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1.2 Deep learning-based methods ‣ 2.1 Image fusion methods ‣ 2 Related work ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the main principle of the proposed FS-Diff. The aligned input images with different <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">clarity</span> conditions are denoted by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{3HW}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{3HW}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{HW}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{HW}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and the HR noise-free fused image, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span> ground truth from CDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite></span> is denoted by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{3HW}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{3HW}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> representing the height and width of the image, respectively. FS-Diff produces the fused <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">result</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> refinement iterations from an image initiated with complete noise: <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. By leveraging the BFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> to construct a joint representation of the inputs  <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x},\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒚</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x},\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the basis of the studied conditional distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}|\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}|\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, FS-Diff iteratively optimizes the output to achieve the sequence output <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{F}_{T-1},\bm{F}_{T-2},\dots,{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\bm{F}_{fused}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{F}_{T-1},\bm{F}_{T-2},\dots,{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\bm{F}_{fused}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and it eventually recovers <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\bm{F}_{fused}}}\sim p(\bm{F}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{{}_{fused}}}|\bm{x},\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑭</mi><mmultiscripts><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\bm{F}_{fused}}}\sim p(\bm{F}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{{}_{fused}}}|\bm{x},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During the iterative refinement process, the distribution of the intermediate images is defined by the forward diffusion process <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{t-1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{t-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, and noise is gradually introduced into each output image via a fixed Markov chain. The goal of FS-Diff is to remove noise based on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and semantic guidance <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{ci}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{ci}</annotation></semantics></math> iteratively by using a reverse Markov chain to recover the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math> with rich semantic information and cross-modal features.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Bidirectional feature Mamba</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To enhance FS-Diff’s ability to extract global and information from source images, we use the BFM </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> block. First, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are concatenated along the channel dimension to form </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, which is fed into the BFM. The structure of the BFM is shown in Figure </span><a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2.1 Bidirectional feature Mamba ‣ 3.2 FS-Diff ‣ 3 Method ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">First, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times C}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is reshaped into patches </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{J\times(P^{2}\cdot C)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">J</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{J\times(P^{2}\cdot C)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, where </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">W</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W,</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> denote the number of channels, height, width, the number of image patches, and the size of image patches, respectively. Then, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are linearly projected to the vector, and positional embeddings </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑬</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are added:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{S}_{0}=[\bm{s}_{\text{cls}};\bm{s}_{p}^{1}\bm{W};\bm{s}_{p}^{2}\bm{W};\dots;\bm{s}_{p}^{j}\bm{W}]+\bm{E}_{p}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑺</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">cls</mtext></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑬</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S}_{0}=[\bm{s}_{\text{cls}};\bm{s}_{p}^{1}\bm{W};\bm{s}_{p}^{2}\bm{W};\dots;\bm{s}_{p}^{j}\bm{W}]+\bm{E}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{(P^{2}\cdot C)\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑾</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">P</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{(P^{2}\cdot C)\times D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is a learnable projection matrix, and </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{p}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{p}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> represents the </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-th patch of </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. The class token </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{s}_{\text{cls}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">cls</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{\text{cls}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> denotes the whole patch sequence.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Next, the token sequence </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{S}_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑺</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S}_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is sent to the Vision Mamba encoder proposed in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">]</span></cite><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to yield </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{S}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑺</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{S}_{l}=\text{BFM}(\bm{S}_{l-1})+\bm{S}_{l-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑺</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">BFM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑺</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑺</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S}_{l}=\text{BFM}(\bm{S}_{l-1})+\bm{S}_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The normalization layer first normalizes </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">S</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and projects it to </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{u}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Then, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is processed in both directions. The 1-D convolution is applied to </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to obtain </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{s}_{o}^{{}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{s}_{o}^{{}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Then, it is linearly projected into </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑪</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{o}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Using </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{o}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\bm{A}}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑨</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\bm{A}}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\bm{B}}_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑩</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">¯</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\bm{B}}_{o}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> are derived. The outputs in the forward and backward directions are subsequently generated via the SSM. These outputs are then gated by </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{u}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and combined to produce the final output token sequence </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{S}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑺</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finally, to provide a more concise mathematical characterization of the BFM’s role, we introduce </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to denote the BFM and </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to represent the global feature representation obtained by the BFM:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}=\Upsilon(\bm{s}_{t}),\quad\bm{s}_{t}=\bm{x}\oplus\bm{y}\oplus\bm{F}_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊕</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊕</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}=\Upsilon(\bm{s}_{t}),\quad\bm{s}_{t}=\bm{x}\oplus\bm{y}\oplus\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where  </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> represents the concatenation operation. The target image with noise is </span><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F_{t}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒕</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝟎</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F_{t}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bm{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
</span><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.2.4" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09427v1/Vim.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/127;" width="476" height="127" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The overview of the BFM.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>CA-CLIP and the CLSE mechanism</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This section introduces the CA-CLIP and the CLSE mechanism. By leveraging CLIP’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite> pre-trained cross-modal alignment capability, our framework enables precise semantic extraction from heterogeneous input modalities with varying clarity. Moreover, this integration enhances the generalizability of the proposed method, enabling its adaptation to more complex real-world environments.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon DA-CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite>, CA-CLIP refines FS-Diff’s semantic understanding of source inputs while regulating the generation process of HR fused results. Its core lies in leveraging CLIP’s pretrained architecture to enable high-quality feature extraction from LR inputs. Simultaneously, it predicts the resolution type of the images (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</span> clear or blur), enabling the model to derive higher-quality semantic information from the clear image when only one of the images is blurred. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1.2 Deep learning-based methods ‣ 2.1 Image fusion methods ‣ 2 Related work ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the structure of CA-CLIP involves the matching of image content embedding <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{ci}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{ci}</annotation></semantics></math> and clear caption embedding <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{ct}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{ct}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, the resolution type embedding <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{ri}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{ri}</annotation></semantics></math> predicted by the image controller specifies the resolution type, which corresponds to the resolution type embedding <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{rt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{rt}</annotation></semantics></math> from the text encoder. The image controller replicates the architecture of the image encoder, incorporating several connections initially set to zero to enhance its control capabilities and adjusting the outputs from each block of the encoder, thereby directing the encoder’s predictive outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the process of simultaneous multimodal image fusion and super-resolution, effectively harnessing semantic information is crucial. The CLIP model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite>, with its pretrained capability to align cross-modal features and extract rich semantic content, plays a pivotal role in computer vision. By integrating CLIP, our framework enables the precise extraction of semantic features from multimodal images. This is achieved even when the images exhibit inconsistent clarity, ultimately aiding in the generation of HR fused images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In our proposed CLSE mechanism, CA-CLIP is first pretrained to acquire clarity-aware capabilities. Given text descriptions, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</span> "clear" and "blur", the corresponding text features <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{rt1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{rt1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{rt2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{rt2}</annotation></semantics></math> are extracted by the pretrained CLIP text encoder. These features are then compared with the image features <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{r1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{r1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{r2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{r2}</annotation></semantics></math>, extracted by the image controller, to assess the semantic similarity between the image and the given text, thereby determining which modality corresponds to the clear image. <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In Case 1, to increase the semantic accuracy of the fused image and reduce the potential errors introduced by the blurred image,</span> the feature of the clear image is extracted through the image encoder and injected into the diffusion process. <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In Case 2, where both images exhibit severe loss of semantic information with only luminance details preserved,</span> the most prominent of the image features obtained from the image encoder for the two images is selected and similarly injected into the diffusion process. <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In addition, the cross-attention mechanism incorporates semantic cues into the fusion and super-resolution process. Here, the feature <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from the BFM serves as the query vector, whereas the semantic cue <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{ci}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{ci}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as both the key and value vectors. The similarity computation between the query and the key reveals regions with high similarity scores, which signify a strong correspondence between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{ci}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{ci}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model then assigns higher attention weights to these regions, thereby improving the recovery of fine details.
</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Diffusion process</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The forward Markovian diffusion process <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> gradually adds Gaussian noise to <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the ground truth</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. At time step <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the noisy output image <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q\left(\bm{F}_{1:T}\middle|\bm{F}_{0}\right)=\prod_{t=1}^{T}q\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{t-1}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\bm{F}_{1:T}\middle|\bm{F}_{0}\right)=\prod_{t=1}^{T}q\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{t-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{t-1}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\bm{F}_{t-1},\left(1-\alpha_{t}\right)\bm{I}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{t-1}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}\bm{F}_{t-1},\left(1-\alpha_{t}\right)\bm{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\alpha_{t}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\alpha_{t}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) represents the hyperparameters that affect the noise variance that needs to be added at each iteration, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unit matrix. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the outputs obtained at time steps <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Notably,</span> the intermediate iterative process describing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">can be marginalized</span> for a given <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> condition:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{0}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\sqrt{\gamma_{t}}\bm{F}_{0},\left(1-\gamma_{t}\right)\bm{I}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\bm{F}_{0}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{F}_{t}\middle|\sqrt{\gamma_{t}}\bm{F}_{0},\left(1-\gamma_{t}\right)\bm{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}=\prod_{i=1}^{T}\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}=\prod_{i=1}^{T}\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is the product of all <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from the initial time step to the current time step <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the HR noise-free fused image</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.4 </span>Optimization of the denoising model</h4>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Training</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">repeat</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x},\bm{y},\bm{F}_{0})\sim p(\bm{x},\bm{y},\bm{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑭</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x},\bm{y},\bm{F}_{0})\sim p(\bm{x},\bm{y},\bm{F})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\gamma_{t}}}\sim p(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\gamma_{t}}}\sim p(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
                Compute the gradient by

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\left\|f_{\theta}\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}}_{t},}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\gamma_{t}}},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}}}\right)-\bm{\varepsilon}\right\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝜺</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\left\|f_{\theta}\left({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}}_{t},}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\gamma_{t}}},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}}}\right)-\bm{\varepsilon}\right\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">until</span> converged

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The neural denoising model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> supplies an approximation of the gradient of the logarithmic density of the data and plays a vital role in FS-Diff. We refine a neural denoising model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the noise <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, in each forward diffusion process and thus perceive the noise level.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To enable FS-Diff to more flexibly incorporate semantic information, we integrate a cross-attention mechanism with U-Net of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The U-Net architecture consists of an encoder-decoder structure connected by a bottleneck. The encoder extracts high-level features, whereas the decoder progressively reconstructs them to the original resolution. Skip connections preserve multilevel features between the encoder and decoder. Noise-level embeddings are incorporated to handle varying noise levels. Semantic conditions are injected into the diffusion process via a cross-attention mechanism, enhancing the semantic consistency of the fused image.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In addition, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{s}}_{t},\gamma_{t},{e}^{ci})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{s}}_{t},\gamma_{t},{e}^{ci})</annotation></semantics></math></span> contains three inputs: the joint global representation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{s}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{s}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,  the noise variance <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the image content embedding <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{ci}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{ci}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Algorithm 1 shows the total training process, the Kaiming initialization method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>]</cite> is used to initialize the conditional noise predictor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The training process begins by sampling the LR image pairs <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x},\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒚</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x},\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and the initial HR target image <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from the joint distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x},\bm{y},\bm{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x},\bm{y},\bm{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. The noise intensity <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is subsequently randomly sampled from the probability distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by sampling noise <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝜺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> from the standard normal distribution. The gradient is then computed based on the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> error between the predicted noise from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the true noise <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝜺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. This iterative process continues until convergence is achieved.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The object function of FS-Diff is denoted as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{L=\mathbb{E}_{\Upsilon(\bm{x},\bm{y},\bm{F}_{t}),\varepsilon,t}\left[\left\|f_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{s}_{t}},\gamma_{t},{e}^{ci})-\bm{\varepsilon}\right\|_{2}^{2}\right]}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">L</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒚</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ε</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝜺</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msubsup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{L=\mathbb{E}_{\Upsilon(\bm{x},\bm{y},\bm{F}_{t}),\varepsilon,t}\left[\left\|f_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{s}_{t}},\gamma_{t},{e}^{ci})-\bm{\varepsilon}\right\|_{2}^{2}\right]}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}=\begin{cases}e^{c1},&amp;\text{if }C_{VI}\land B_{IR},\\
e^{c2},&amp;\text{if }C_{IR}\land B_{VI},\\
\max(e^{c1},e^{c2}),&amp;\text{if }B_{VI}\land B_{IR},\end{cases}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∧</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∧</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></msup><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∧</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}=\begin{cases}e^{c1},&amp;\text{if }C_{VI}\land B_{IR},\\
e^{c2},&amp;\text{if }C_{IR}\land B_{VI},\\
\max(e^{c1},e^{c2}),&amp;\text{if }B_{VI}\land B_{IR},\end{cases}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where  <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">It is added to the HR target image to generate a noisy version, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to predict this noise.</span>  <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the expected value. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{VI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{VI}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{IR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">B</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{IR}</annotation></semantics></math> denote blurred VI and IR, respectively, whereas <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{VI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{VI}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{IR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">R</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{IR}</annotation></semantics></math> represent clear VI and IR, respectively. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{c1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{c1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{c2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{c2}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the features of VI and IR, respectively, from the image encoder of a pretrained CA-CLIP. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\land" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∧</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\land</annotation></semantics></math> denotes that the conditions are satisfied simultaneously.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.5 </span>Inference</h4>

<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> The inference of FS-Diff</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{T}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=T,T-1,\ldots,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=T,T-1,\ldots,1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}=\bm{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}=\bm{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\bm{F}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\gamma_{t}}}f_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}}_{t},}}\gamma_{t},e^{ci})\right)+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t-1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\bm{F}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\gamma_{t}}}f_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}}_{t},}}\gamma_{t},e^{ci})\right)+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The inference process of FS-Diff is defined as a reverse Markovian process, as opposed to a forward diffusion process. Our goal was to model the distribution of <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the fused result <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the given <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> conditions. That is, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p(}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\bm{F}_{fused}}}\mid\bm{x},\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p(}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\bm{F}_{fused}}}\mid\bm{x},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math>. The detailed computational procedure for the inference is described in Algorithm 2, which starts with the Gaussian noise <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{F}_{T})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{F}_{T}\mid\bm{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{F}_{T})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{F}_{T}\mid\bm{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For each time step <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to 1), if <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, a random noise vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathcolor="#000000">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝟎</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑰</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>=1, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0. Same as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, the vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the noise introduced during the inference process. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝜺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is subsequently estimated via <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is iteratively refined. Ultimately, this iterative process yields the final HR fused image, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">. Each iteration generates output images <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}\rightarrow\bm{F}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}\rightarrow\bm{F}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with different noise levels, and as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, the noise level decreases. At the end of the iteration, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math> is returned as the final clear fusion result, the mathematical expression is as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{0:T}\mid\bm{x},\bm{y})=p(\bm{F}_{T})\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}\mid\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{0:T}\mid\bm{x},\bm{y})=p(\bm{F}_{T})\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}\mid\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}\mid\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{F}_{t-1}\mid\mu_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{,e^{ci}}}),\sigma_{t}^{2}\bm{I})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}\mid\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})=\mathcal{N}(\bm{F}_{t-1}\mid\mu_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{,e^{ci}}}),\sigma_{t}^{2}\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}=\frac{(1-\gamma_{t-1})(1-\alpha_{t})}{1-\gamma_{t}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}=\frac{(1-\gamma_{t-1})(1-\alpha_{t})}{1-\gamma_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{,e^{ci}}})=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\bm{F}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\gamma_{t}}}f_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}},}}\gamma_{t},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}}})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{,e^{ci}}})=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\bm{F}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\gamma_{t}}}f_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}},}}\gamma_{t},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{,e^{ci}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{,e^{ci}}})</annotation></semantics></math> represent the variance and the mean of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}\mid\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\bm{F}_{t-1}\mid\bm{F}_{t},\bm{x},\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to predict <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated under the given condition for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{F}}_{fused}}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\gamma_{t}}}\left(\bm{F}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\gamma_{t}}f_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}}}))\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{F}}_{fused}}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\gamma_{t}}}\left(\bm{F}_{t}-\sqrt{1-\gamma_{t}}f_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}}}))\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\bm{F}}}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\bm{F}}}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math> is the approximation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{fused}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝑭</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{fused}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the refinement steps for each iteration are as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t-1}\leftarrow\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\bm{F}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\gamma_{t}}}f_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}}})\right)+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\bm{\varepsilon}_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒔</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝜺</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t-1}\leftarrow\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{t}}}\left(\bm{F}_{t}-\frac{1-\alpha_{t}}{\sqrt{1-\gamma_{t}}}f_{\theta}({\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{\hat{\bm{s}_{t}}}},\gamma_{t},{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}{e^{ci}}})\right)+\sqrt{1-\alpha_{t}}\bm{\varepsilon}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜺</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\bm{I})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Multimodality image fusion benchmark</h2>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09427v1/03.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/143;" width="476" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>(a) Image pair acquisition, (b) post-processing, and (c) scene distribution for AVMS. </figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09427v1/04.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/133;" width="476" height="133" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Viewable distribution of scenes in our AVMS dataset. The first line is the visible images, and the second line is the corresponding infrared images. The text in the green box represents the scene type.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal datasets are crucial for building accurate and efficient deep learning-based MMIF systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. However, most existing multimodal image datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> mainly rely on handheld IR or VI cameras, which have a relatively limited image field of view. In real-world complex environments, such as battlefield <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">environments</span>, UAVs need to adjust the shooting distance and camera parameters in real time to cope with changing terrain and weather, often resulting in the loss of semantic information and local structural features. This significantly affects the accuracy of advanced vision tasks.
<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In recent years, several aerial datasets have been proposed for specific tasks, such as vehicle detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, drone tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and broader object detection tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Anti-UAV410 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> provides a thermal IR UAV tracking benchmark covering lakes, forests, and mountains, offering valuable support for anti-UAV tracking algorithm development. However, it is limited to IR images and does not support multimodal image fusion. VEDAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> focuses on small vehicle detection from an aerial perspective, including both large and small-sized IR and VI image pairs. Nonetheless, its limited illumination conditions and few nighttime scenes restrict its application in low-light scenarios. DVTOD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite> introduces a misaligned visible-thermal dataset with 54 scenes and 16 challenging attributes, but the misalignment adds complexity to the multimodal fusion algorithm design. DroneVehicle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> provides a drone-based RGB-IR image pair for vehicle detection under varying lighting conditions from day to night, yet its narrow target category limits its use in broader tasks.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Although these datasets contribute significantly to object detection and tracking, they fail to support the comprehensive training and evaluation of multimodal fusion algorithms</span> <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To address these challenges</span>, we established the AVMS dataset, which has a wide range of shooting angles, flexible shooting angles, and diverse lighting conditions. Unlike existing datasets, AVMS incorporates various scenes with multiple shooting distances, providing data support for developing practical deep-learning-based <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">MMIF</span> and super-resolution methods. AVMS is expected to be embedded in unmanned aerial systems with multisource heterogeneous data fusion functions in the future, which will provide key technical support for intelligent battlefields and UAV crop inspection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A professional UAV was used for image acquisition, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Multimodality image fusion benchmark ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a)). The UAV can fly and shoot steadily in windy, snowy, rainy, and low-light conditions to obtain the correct target size, and the drone can be controlled at an altitude of 5–20 m. The standard resolutions of the captured images were 1280<math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>1024 and 4000<math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>3000. To address the issue of the inconsistent field-of-view size of the built-in camera of the UAV, we cropped and registered the VI and IR images, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Multimodality image fusion benchmark ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b). In addition, to obtain aligned image pairs with <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">different clarity</span>, the two modal images were downsampled via bicubic interpolation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The 859 pairs of images contained in the AVMS dataset were classified into four categories on the basis of illumination levels: daytime, dusk, nighttime, and complex weather. Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 Multimodality image fusion benchmark ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows eight subscenes: residences, roads, campuses, parks, streets, stadiums, parking lots, croplands, and diverse environments in both urban and rural areas. Notably, MSIF aims to improve the accuracy of advanced vision tasks, such as detection and segmentation, to increase the effectiveness of applications in real-world scenarios, such as autonomous driving and drone combat. To this end, the AVMS dataset provides 3,821 annotated targets, including 3,407 cars, 60 buses, 3 grabs, 63 trucks, 42 electric vehicles, 188 people, 56 tents, and 2 boats, laying a critical data foundation for subsequent target detection and semantic segmentation tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section is organized as follows. Subsection <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experimental setups ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> details the experimental setup. Subsections <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 IR and VI image fusion ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Medical image fusion ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> outline comparative experiments with state-of-the-art methods on the VIRF and MIF tasks, respectively, and provide quantitative versus qualitative analyses. Subsection <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Ablation experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> explores the validity of the BFM block and <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">CLSE mechanism</span> via ablation experiments. Subsection <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Performance on advanced visual task ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> evaluates the performance of the model on an advanced visual task. Finally, Subsection <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Extended experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a> outlines extended FS-Diff experiments on multifocus image fusion.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental setups</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1) Datasets:</span> Our experiments were conducted using four VIRF datasets: LLVIP (training: 12025, test: 3463) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, M<sup id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>FD (training: 3780, test: 300) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, MSRS (training: 1083, test: 362) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and our AVMS (training: 689 ,test: 170 ). MRI-PET (training: 287, test: 32) and MRI-SPECT (training: 497, test: 55) of the Harvard medical image dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>]</cite> were selected for the MIF experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2) Image processing:</span> The HR fusion image was obtained via CDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and the LR source image was down-sampled from the HR source image via a bicubic <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">approach</span> to synthesize the “LR multimodal image-HR fusion image” training set. For the test set, similar to the configuration in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">106</a>, <a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>, <a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">108</a>]</cite>, we cropped the three public datasets to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>, degraded them bicubically, and named them LLVIP-val, M<sup id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>FD-val, and MSRS-val.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3) Compared methods: </span> We compared FS-Diff with 10 state-of-the-art (SOTA) image fusion approaches whose codes are publicly available in a VIRF task. We classified them into five groups. The first group is AE-based methods, and includes CDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, UMF<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">and PromptFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>]</cite></span>. The second group is CNN-based methods and consists of <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">CoCoNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>,</span> Reconet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, IGNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>]</cite>, and LRRNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>. The third group is GAN-based methods and includes Tardal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> and TGFuse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">111</a>]</cite>. The fourth group is diffusion model-based methods and includes DIF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite> and DDFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. The fifth group is MetaFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, a SOTA joint image super-resolution and fusion method. <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Moreover</span>, we compared FS-Diff with SOTA methods, including CDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, BDLFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, U2Fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, and DDFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> which are applicable to the MIF task, to prove its effectiveness in MIF.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4) Metrics:</span> To objectively and comprehensively evaluate the fused results, visual information fidelity (VIF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">112</a>]</cite>, Q<sup id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_sup">AB/F</sup> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">113</a>]</cite>, structural similarity index measure (SSIM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">114</a>]</cite>, peak signal to noise ratio (PSNR), learned perceptual image patch similarity (LPIPS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite>, and mean squared error (MSE) were used. The VIF, SSIM, and PSNR are image fidelity measures, whereas Q<sup id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_sup">AB/F</sup> is evaluated by quantifying the edge information, LPIPS measures the perceived quality of the image, and MSE <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">corresponds</span> to the error in predicting the image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5) Implementation details:</span> <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Our model was trained on four NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPUs and tested on a single RTX 3090 GPU.</span> <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">During training, we considered various scaling factors,</span> including <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\times 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>𝟐</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\times 2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\times 4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>𝟒</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\times 4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\times 8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>𝟖</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\times 8}</annotation></semantics></math>, for a total of 800,000 training steps with a batch size of 80. The Adam optimizer was adopted with a fixed learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Adapting the parameter configuration proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">116</a>]</cite>, we employ <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=4000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">4000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=4000</annotation></semantics></math> to maintain an optimal trade-off between fusion accuracy and inference speed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">With the exception of MetaFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, other comparison methods cannot be directly applied to the joint task of fusion and super-resolution. Therefore, these methods perform super-resolution followed by fusion in a stepwise manner for comparison with FS-Diff. <span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Among various image super-resolution methods<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>, <a href="#bib.bib117" title="" class="ltx_ref">117</a>]</cite>, given the superior performance of SR3 across multiple scaling factors, we selected it as the baseline for super-resolution.</span> Specifically, LR source image pairs were initially super-resolved to obtain HR images via SR3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>]</cite>, which <span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">was</span> retrained on MMIF images, and then these HR images <span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">were</span> fused to obtain the final fused image.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>IR and VI image fusion</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1 </span>Subjective assessment</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.2.2 Objective assessment ‣ 5.2 IR and VI image fusion ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the fusion and super-resolution results of several SOTA methods with relatively better visual performance at the <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\times 8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>𝟖</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\times 8}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\times 4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>𝟒</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\times 4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\times 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>𝟐</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\times 2}</annotation></semantics></math> scales. As seen from the enlarged area, the compared methods exhibit a degree of blurriness and a deficiency in fine-grained details and fail to retain the structures of "person" in LLVIP and MSRS, "electrical pole" in M<sup id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>FD and "cars" in AVMS.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The fusion results of IGNet+SR3, DIF+SR3, and DDFM+SR3 on the LLVIP dataset exhibit significant semantic errors in reconstructing "person" and "car". <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Compared with the other methods, CoCoNet+SR3 and PromptFusion+SR3 exhibit good detail preservation of "person" in the LLVIP and MSRS datasets.</span> Moreover, CDD+SR3, IGNet+SR3, Tardal+SR3, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">and CoCoNet+SR3</span> demonstrate artifacts in smooth areas, such as roadways in the M<sup id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>FD dataset. Conversely, FS-Diff demonstrates greater stability in smooth areas and preserves richer contrast and significant information , highlighting its notable advantages in various fusion and super-resolution scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2 </span>Objective assessment</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2.2 Objective assessment ‣ 5.2 IR and VI image fusion ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2.2 Objective assessment ‣ 5.2 IR and VI image fusion ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> show the VIF, Q<sup id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">AB/F</sup>, SSIM, PSNR, LPIPS, and MSE values for VIRF and super-resolution on three public datasets and our constructed datasets, under three different resolution degradation scenarios of the source images. The following observations were made: (1) CDD+SR3 and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PromptFusion+SR3</span> demonstrate strong performance in terms of VIF and PSNR, effectively preserving visual information fidelity. Reconet+SR3 and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">DDFM+SR3</span> <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">outperform UMF+SR3 in terms of the MSE</span>. On the LLVIP dataset, LRRNet+SR3 achieves commendable PSNR scores. In the context of blurred IR images at a <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> scaling factor, TGFuse+SR3 <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">and PromptFusion+SR3</span> show robust overall performance. By contrast, DIF+SR3, IGNet+SR3, and Tardal+SR3 yield relatively average results. (2) As a SOTA joint image fusion and super-resolution method, MetaFusion is not capable of efficiently fusing images with severely degraded resolution, and performs poorly on six metrics. (3) FS-Diff performed well for the VIF, Q<sup id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_sup">AB/F</sup>, SSIM, and LPIPS metrics. Overall, FS-Diff, which integrates both image fusion and super-resolution tasks in a unified framework, demonstrates superior performance across all four datasets compared with methods that process these tasks separately. This fully demonstrates its ability to generate high-resolution fused images that are consistent with the visual perception of the human eye, with a more complete retention of details and semantic information.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09427v1/05-842t.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/690;" width="476" height="690" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Fusion and super-resolution (scale:8, 4 and 2) results for different methods on <span id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">LLVIP, M<sup id="S5.F6.3.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>FD, MSRS and</span> our AVMS datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Subjective and objective evaluations simultaneously affirmed the applicability of FS-Diff for image fusion and super-resolution at different magnifications in different scenes. This is primarily because FS-Diff is trained based on joint multitask learning, including fusion and super-resolution, which strengthens the feature recognition ability of the model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>The objective results for infrared and visible image fusion and super resolution (<math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\times 8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.222em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>𝟖</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\times 8}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Red</span>: the best, <span id="S5.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Blue</span>: the second, <span id="S5.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">Green</span>: the third).</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:323.1pt;vertical-align:-160.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-213.8pt,200.2pt) scale(0.446554584163681,0.446554584163681) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
