<figure id="S5.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09427v1/focus.png" id="S5.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>
Visual Comparison of SR3-only super-resolution and the proposed method for simultaneous multi-source image fusion and super-resolution (scale:8).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F14" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09427v1/10.png" id="S5.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>Fusion and super-resolution (scale: 8) results for different methods on the Lytro and MFI multi-focus datasets. (a1)–(a2) and (h1)–(h2) are partially focused source images; (b1)–(b2) and (i1)–(i2) are input images obtained via bicubic downsampling; (c1)–(g1) and (j1)–(n1) are fused results; (c2)–(g2) are the differences produced by (c1)–(g1) with (a2); and (j2)–(n2) are the differences produced by (j1)–(n1) with (h2).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Existing multi-focus image fusion methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">120</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib121" title="" class="ltx_ref">121</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib122" title="" class="ltx_ref">122</a>, <a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite> are limited to fixed resolutions. Although strategies such as performing super-resolution prior to fusion can be employed, the former may introduce artifacts that, when amplified in subsequent steps, significantly degrade the final result. Simultaneous super-resolution and fusion of multifocus images enhance the information content in the fused result, preserving more focused features from the source images.</span> To further validate the generalizability of FS-Diff, we compared it with FusionDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">120</a>]</cite>, RRSTD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, MUFusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib121" title="" class="ltx_ref">121</a>]</cite>, and U2Fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> on the public datasets Lytro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib122" title="" class="ltx_ref">122</a>]</cite> and MFI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite>. The stepwise fusion and super-resolution methods were used for comparison. For training, HR fusion images were obtained via U2Fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and training image pairs were obtained by bicubically downsampling the source images. To be consistent with the above experiment, we centered and cropped the two datasets, degraded them to <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>, and termed them Lytro-val and MFI-val, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The quantitative evaluation under the condition where both image
modalities are blurred in Table <a href="#S5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 5.6 Extended experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows that FS-Diff achieved the best values for the VIF, Q<sup id="S5.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup">AB/F</sup>, SSIM and LPIPS metrics, with RRSTD closely following. Compared <span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">with</span> the SR3-only (far/near) method, which applies super-resolution to the two source images, FS-Diff has significant advantages in preserving visual information fidelity, structural similarity, fusion quality, and <span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">visual</span> consistency. <span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 5.6 Extended experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, despite the super-resolution processing of the low-resolution image (Figures <a href="#S5.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 5.6 Extended experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> (a3)-(a4),
(b3)-(b4)), the out-of-focus information remains arduous to recover (Figures <a href="#S5.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 5.6 Extended experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> (a5)-(a6), (b5)-(b6)).</span> For a more intuitive visual evaluation, Figures <a href="#S5.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 5.6 Extended experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> (c2)-(g2) and (j8)-(n2) show the differences obtained using the different methods. Less information in the difference map is obtained by subtracting the focus area in one source image from the fusion image, implying that more focus information is extracted by the fusion method. <span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The</span> enlarged area in the lower right corner of Figure <a href="#S5.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 5.6 Extended experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, FusionDiff, MUFusion, NSST, and U2Fusion have different levels of residuals around the edge of the hat of the “child” and around the ears of the “lady with the umbrella.” In addition, FusionDiff’s stepwise fusion and super-resolution for the “lady with the umbrella” is unsatisfactory, with a <span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">severe</span> loss of semantic information. By contrast, Figures <a href="#S5.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 5.6 Extended experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ FS-Diff: Semantic Guidance and Clarity-Aware Simultaneous Multimodal Image Fusion and Super-Resolution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> (g2) and (n2) show the most continuous and cleanest hat and ear edges, respectively, and preserve the continuous boundary information.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Computational complexity analysis</h2>

<figure id="S6.T10" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>Comparison of computational complexity and inference time</figcaption>
<div id="S6.T10.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:52.7pt;vertical-align:-25.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-148.6pt,22.7pt) scale(0.537265889599857,0.537265889599857) ;">
<table id="S6.T10.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T10.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T10.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Methods</td>
<td id="S6.T10.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">CDD<span id="S6.T10.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">+SR3</span></td>
<td id="S6.T10.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Reconet<span id="S6.T10.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">+SR3</span></td>
<td id="S6.T10.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">IGNet<span id="S6.T10.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">+SR3</span></td>
<td id="S6.T10.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">UMF<span id="S6.T10.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">+SR3</span></td>
<td id="S6.T10.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">LRRNet<span id="S6.T10.2.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">+SR3</span></td>
<td id="S6.T10.2.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Tardal<span id="S6.T10.2.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">+SR3</span></td>
<td id="S6.T10.2.1.1.8" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">TGFuse<span id="S6.T10.2.1.1.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">+SR3</span></td></tr>
